 ### [Entry Point Adjustment](7406-entry-point-adjustment.md)
-Entry Point Adjustment is a hardware-accelerated volume rendering technique that enables the camera to move inside the volume's bounding box without the view disappearing or clipping artifacts. It solves the problem of ray termination when the camera position exceeds the front face of the volume by dynamically shifting the ray's entry point forward along the ray direction based on a scale coefficient. Unlike standard ray casting where the entry point is fixed at the front face, this method allows for a "flowing through" effect, distinguishing it from sibling methods like [Back-face rasterized volume rendering](1296-back-face-rasterized-volume-rendering.md) which rely on static front-face rasterization. Choose this method when navigating inside the volume data is required.
